\vskip 18pt

\section {Introduction}

This document is typeset more than once. On the first pass the engine learns
where every numbered heading lands; on the next it fills in the table of contents
above and the references below. Forward references work because of it: the
conclusion is \autoref{sec:conclusion}, which begins on page~\pageref{sec:conclusion},
and the worked example is \autoref{sec:example} on page~\pageref{sec:example}.

\label{sec:intro}A reference can also point backward, of course --- this
introduction is \autoref{sec:intro}, titled ``\nameref{sec:intro}''.

\section {Background}

\label{sec:background}A cross-reference binds a name to a numbered thing with \texttt{label}, then names
it later with \texttt{ref} (the number), \texttt{pageref} (the page), \texttt{autoref}
(the number with its kind word in front, as above), or \texttt{nameref} (the title).
The number is captured where the label is written; the page is learned when that
page ships, so neither has to be known in advance.

\subsection {How the table of contents is built}

Each sectioning command quietly records its level, number, title and page. The
\texttt{tableofcontents} command replays those entries, drawing a dotted leader
from each title to its page number --- the classic use of leaders. Figures and
tables file their captions into separate lists, replayed by \texttt{listoffigures}
and \texttt{listoftables}.

\figure[h]{
\centerline{\includegraphics[width=0.30\linewidth]{frog.jpg}}
\vskip 6pt
\centerline{\caption{A frog, the running example of an image float.}\label{fig:frog}}
}

\autoref{fig:frog} shows an image float; its caption is filed in the list of
figures at the top. A display equation can be numbered and referenced the same way:
$$ e^{i\pi} + 1 = 0 \eqno 1 $$
\label{eq:euler}so \eqref{eq:euler} above is Euler's identity.

\vfill\eject

\section {A worked example}

\label{sec:example}This section sits on its own page so its number and folio are
worth referencing from the introduction. Look back at the contents: this is
\autoref{sec:example}, and the background discussion was \autoref{sec:background}.

\table[h]{
\tabular{ll}{\texttt{ref} & the number \cr \texttt{pageref} & the page \cr \texttt{autoref} & the number with its kind \cr \texttt{nameref} & the title \cr}
\vskip 6pt
\caption{The reference commands, for the list of tables.}\label{tab:one}
}

\autoref{tab:one} is filed in the list of tables. We can also point at it by name:
``\nameref{tab:one}''.

\vfill\eject

\section {Conclusion}

\label{sec:conclusion}Forward and backward references, a table of contents,
and separate lists of figures and tables --- with real page numbers --- all fall
out of typesetting the document twice over one shared table. This conclusion is
\autoref{sec:conclusion} on page~\pageref{sec:conclusion}.

\vfill\eject
